course: dictionary
lesson: 63
title: Feeling words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Achy describes a dull, steady pain, often in the muscles or joints. You can feel achy when you are tired, when you are ill with a cold or flu, or after a lot of exercise. «Staying in one position for a long time, sitting or lying, makes muscles stiff and achy.» The clue is stiff: achy is how muscles feel after they have been still for too long.
explain: Words around achy give you more clues. When someone is ill, their whole body can feel achy, so a sentence about a cold or flu tells you the pain is spread out, not in one sharp spot. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Achy is an everyday, informal word, the kind you hear when people talk about how they feel. Telling a grown-up "my legs feel achy" helps them understand what is wrong and look after you.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Achy» means feeling a dull, steady pain, often in the muscles or joints.
fact: «Achy» is an adjective, and its forms are achy, achier and achiest.
fact: «Achy» is informal, so you will hear it in everyday talk more than in formal writing.
fact: You can feel achy after exercise, when you are tired, or when you are ill with a cold or flu.
exercise: «Staying in one position for a long time makes muscles stiff and achy.» What does achy mean here? | *A dull, steady pain in the muscles | A loud noise from far away | A bright and happy feeling | A sudden, sharp cut
exercise: After a long football match, Sam says, «My legs feel achy.» What is he telling us? | He is very hungry for his dinner | His shoes are too big | He has just won the match | *His legs have a dull, steady pain
exercise: Which is the best reason to say you feel achy? | You have a new pair of trainers | *You have a cold and your muscles hurt all over | You have just finished reading a book | You have been given a prize
test: «My back feels achy after gardening all morning.» Is this pain sharp and sudden, or dull and steady? | Loud and noisy | *Dull and steady | Bright and shiny | Sharp and sudden
test: Ella says she feels achy and has a runny nose. What is most likely true? | She has a brand new bike | She has just won a race | She is on holiday by the sea | *She is probably unwell
exam: Is achy a formal or informal word, and what does it describe? | *Informal, and it describes a dull, steady pain | Informal, and it describes a loud noise | Formal, and it describes a sudden, sharp cut | Formal, and it describes a bright colour
